#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087601 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "8ba9598f-a266-57cb-8ef5-9757dd612a34" :question/math-academy-id "e-6607" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} -4 \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} +4 y = 8x + 4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - 4\\lambda + 4 = 0\n$$\n\nhas a repeated root $\\lambda =  2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ { 2 x} + B x e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $1,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $1.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p=\\alpha x + \\beta\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-4\\alpha +4(\\alpha x+\\beta ) & =8x+4 \\\\ 4\\alpha x+(4\\beta -4\\alpha ) & =8x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4\\alpha =8\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 4\\beta -4\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=2, \\beta=3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p$ is\n\n$$\ny_p =2x+3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{2x}+Bx{e}^{2x}+2x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "1b78a582-0bfa-53dc-b392-99b7da223ff6" :question/math-academy-id "e-6608" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 + \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = 1,-2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a polynomial of degree $2,$ a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $2.$ So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha x^2 + \\beta x + \\gamma\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma$ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_p' = 2\\alpha x\n+\\beta,\\qquad y_p'' = 2\\alpha.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha,$ $\\beta,$ and $\\gamma,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha +(2\\alpha x+\\beta )-2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3 \\\\ -2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha -2\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma ) & ={x}^{2}+5x-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha =1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha -2\\beta =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta -2\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha=-\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\beta=-3, \\gamma=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p =- \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** We write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{-2x}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-3x-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087609 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/id #uuid "83788240-c717-5e86-8753-ca320f4c979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6721" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm {d} ^ 2 y} {\\textrm {d} x ^ 2} - \\dfrac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y = 6.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "**Step 1:** First we find the complementary solution $y_c$ by solving the homogeneous ODE. The characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 2 = 0\n$$\n\nhas roots $\\lambda = -1, 2,$ so the complementary solution is\n\n$$\ny_c = A e ^ {- x} + B e ^ {2 x}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Second, we find a particular solution $y_p$ of the inhomogeneous ODE. Since the right hand side is a constant, it is a polynomial of degree $0,$ which means a particular solution will also be a polynomial of degree $0,$ or a constant. So we assume\n\n$$\ny_p =\\alpha,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ is a constant to be found. Calculating the first and second derivatives of $y_p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = 0,\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2y_p}{\\textrm{d}x^2} = 0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha,$ we substitute the derivatives into the original ODE and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-0-2\\alpha =6\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny_p = -3.\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Finally, we write the general solution as the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={y}_{c}+{y}_{p} \\\\  & =A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{2x}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087613 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "25b04b8f-7447-572f-8600-9d11e5423c2c" :question/math-academy-id "e-6786" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{x} + Be^{- 2 x} - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\ny'' +y' -2y = x ^ 2 + 5 x - 3,\n\\qquad y(0) =  -\\dfrac{1}{2}, \\ \\ y'(0) = 0.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $A$ and $B$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0) = -\\dfrac{1}{2}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+B -\\dfrac{1}{2}  = -\\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition $y'(0) = 0,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\ny' = A e^{x} -2 B e^{-2 x} -x -3,\n$$\n\nand then we substitute $y'(0)=0$ into the above to get\n\n$$\nA-2B -3 = 0.\n$$\n\nSo we have the following system of equations for $A$ and $B\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}A+B=0 \\\\ A-2B=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=1, B=-1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny = e^{x} -e^{-2 x}  - \\frac{x^2}{2} - 3 x - \\dfrac 1 2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd4ae9bf-e3a6-537f-b879-0d017689ae69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086023} {:answer/id #uuid "beeff81b-4a2e-5b71-ad37-881a6c2f762d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-2x + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086024} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a8f2ec-1a51-5b51-af67-3a73d8ad9a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086025} {:answer/id #uuid "fe31c316-6048-5fa9-8982-3ec330d49714" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} =-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086026} {:answer/id #uuid "3ce4089c-3944-5fe4-a22b-6294b77d033a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y_{p} = 4x - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086027}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e9d1e661-86cd-5c5e-bccb-65d2d5c5f0e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186086022}] :question/id #uuid "4a601b5b-b013-5c42-bdd5-a25b3a11ff4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-64284" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 2\\frac{dy}{dx} - 8y =-16$\nhas the complementary function $y_{c}(x) = Ae^{4x} + Be^{-2x}$, find the particular solution $y_{p}(x)$ to this equation."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5720f24-4834-5f92-98ef-62d15aa34022" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=\\frac{x}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403126} {:answer/id #uuid "94298549-9f11-59e3-9778-348dfb75c6dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=2x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403127} {:answer/id #uuid "a894006f-0a61-540c-bca8-073ac57ffb84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403128} {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129} {:answer/id #uuid "9f49a9b7-19a3-54bd-a9db-ec8551633352" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98f1d505-2a8f-586d-b7f5-1495b711f3f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403129} :answer-field/id #uuid "090c78d3-3a0c-5fa4-b1fc-517e968e51e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403125}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d511e4f-8803-5021-b5c2-53a7b4de5acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-64285" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-5y=-5x+6\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-4\\alpha -5(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n-5\\alpha x+(-4\\alpha -5\\beta )=-5x+6\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5\\alpha =-5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -4\\alpha -5\\beta =6\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21a85e00-002e-526f-aa68-9fa9a9e3cd49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+5x+8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342517} {:answer/id #uuid "3f517e31-a1d3-5caa-8710-7ee592d9e883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-2{x}^{2}+4x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342760} {:answer/id #uuid "565ce293-b429-5b97-aecf-cf9c7e935987" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342957} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3d97f-80c2-5792-afc6-911099c236aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343698} {:answer/id #uuid "e06fa21a-8c01-5a09-8cc4-778680199c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+3x-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5f3d97f-80c2-5792-afc6-911099c236aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343698} :answer-field/id #uuid "84373811-cac1-5c50-acaa-26cce938f12f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344726}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a92cf087-3d29-5414-a809-a999e198051b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77763" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{2x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -3(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\beta -6\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma )=4{x}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\beta -6\\alpha =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -3\\beta +2\\gamma =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =6,\\,\\gamma =6.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+6x+6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53031c9e-3029-5c89-b063-5ccb942ef9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+4{e}^{3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346059} {:answer/id #uuid "8c2eaf7f-fcdb-5fea-8828-d7c11a09e69b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346567} {:answer/id #uuid "b71804c0-e438-5968-a64c-04648858fbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346947} {:answer/id #uuid "c9ec8f13-952d-5419-9dbe-a75028b116ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}+{e}^{3x}-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347113} {:answer/id #uuid "d8decbe4-3b1e-515c-be19-386620836e39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{e}^{-3x}+4x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b71804c0-e438-5968-a64c-04648858fbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346947} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ec4db00-a997-5e7a-a546-2f8ffac2ce10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "870e7b8e-206b-50c2-8d89-7f75a9431965" :question/math-academy-id "q-7901" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=3,\\,y(0)=\\frac{4}{3},\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+6{y}^{′}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=0,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $A$ and $B.$ \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{4}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nA+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}.\n$$\n\nThus, $A=1.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=5.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=(B-3A){e}^{-3x}-3Bx{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=5$ into the above gives\n\n$$\nB-3A=5.\n$$\n\nSo we have $A=1$ and $B=8.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}+8x{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088364 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51c26de1-64db-53ff-a3b5-4cb2aa7cbbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346055} {:answer/id #uuid "834b955e-0e91-5d48-93b7-078f0a7d9b7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346494} {:answer/id #uuid "b6361fcf-d8fb-5366-b2bd-4a6d76859ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346938} {:answer/id #uuid "c131fa8e-a30c-5300-a468-a2856e033e21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347021} {:answer/id #uuid "f7a12b0d-40e2-5ccc-9c14-7c223f437b0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{4x}+B{e}^{-4x}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c26de1-64db-53ff-a3b5-4cb2aa7cbbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346055} :answer-field/id #uuid "35c068d9-5cbe-5ce9-a70e-f7047cf5378b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347696}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "513cfc62-eb9a-585a-a155-a58861456868" :question/math-academy-id "q-7902" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=32x+16.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+8\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+16y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+8\\lambda +16 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +4)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =4$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n0+8\\alpha +16(\\alpha x+\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n16\\alpha x+(8\\alpha +16\\beta )=32x+16\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}16\\alpha =32\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 8\\alpha +16\\beta =16\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =2,\\,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-4x}+Bx{e}^{-4x}+2x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded875ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345360} {:answer/id #uuid "03917c72-354d-53bd-8eb1-b175d01190d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345383} {:answer/id #uuid "0e6f8c3a-e267-5331-9844-155baa68208c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x+\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345469} {:answer/id #uuid "1ba85172-106c-541a-a67c-8d14ff82d144" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+B{e}^{-5x}+{x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345590} {:answer/id #uuid "7677da2d-0340-547c-ba24-118238fab432" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346392}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00e011e8-4a7a-508d-bca5-385ded875ecb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345360} :answer-field/id #uuid "92191c63-14bc-50e1-b8ca-0152f65d00c6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "445996cc-ad9d-514f-8bb7-d053c9192103" :question/math-academy-id "q-7903" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=25{x}^{2}+5x-3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-10\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+25y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =5$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha -10(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+25(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n25\\alpha {x}^{2}+(25\\beta -20\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma )=25{x}^{2}+5x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}25\\alpha =25\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 25\\beta -20\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha -10\\beta +25\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =\\frac{1}{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}+Bx{e}^{5x}+{x}^{2}+x+\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46b61525-d20a-548a-b756-9e59c5c5df90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}+2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371100} {:answer/id #uuid "d804cefe-dc65-5653-bac7-1ee49d119f27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}+x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371101} {:answer/id #uuid "dd45f29b-ae24-5c96-b86f-5216971fb4bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=3{x}^{2}-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371102} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3bac32-61c1-54c0-8921-9bc005fa5675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)=2{x}^{2}-x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371103} {:answer/id #uuid "fe6182a3-ea22-5ea5-8440-04b1c46b656c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe6182a3-ea22-5ea5-8440-04b1c46b656c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371104} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fd83c91-8d54-5d91-bd2e-603f01529164" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a9cf2e1e-2a40-5120-879a-34cfa64cf58d" :question/math-academy-id "q-79616" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+4\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha \n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +4(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+3(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha {x}^{2}+(8\\alpha +3\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma )=3{x}^{2}+2x+3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 8\\alpha +3\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +4\\beta +3\\gamma =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =1,\\beta =-2,\\gamma =3.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={x}^{2}-2x+3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088381 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ab841b6-2b4a-5ee2-b66b-fcf171e89172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345991} {:answer/id #uuid "5cc448e1-0a31-5de7-9fe2-10f49c01aecf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346144} {:answer/id #uuid "c72c0a78-04f7-5822-9ad8-21c54916589a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347082} {:answer/id #uuid "f3e9726e-70b0-54ca-8265-d30ce7498043" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+{x}^{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347510} {:answer/id #uuid "f9515ac3-2583-53df-adc9-71966a869081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347554}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9515ac3-2583-53df-adc9-71966a869081" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347554} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2c7102b-1630-5782-a186-4a8f63eeea61" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddb29a9d-408f-5d53-82cb-356f77e1a234" :question/math-academy-id "q-8034" :question/problem "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=13{x}^{2}+15x-24,\\,y(0)=2,\\text{ }\\text{ }{y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+26y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +26 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1)-1+26 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}+25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2} & =-25 \\\\ \\lambda +1 & =\\pm 5\\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-1\\pm 5\\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral, ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,{y}_{p}^{″}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+26(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24.\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n26\\alpha {x}^{2}+(26\\beta +4\\alpha )x+(2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma )=13{x}^{2}+15x-24\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}26\\alpha =13\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 26\\beta +4\\alpha =15\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +2\\beta +26\\gamma =-24\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =\\frac{1}{2},\\gamma =-1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$\n\nNow let's find the constants $P$ and $Q.$  \n\nSubstituting $y(0)=2$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\nP-1=2.\n$$\n\nThus, $P=3.$\n\nWe now need to apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}.$ First, we differentiate $y$ to find ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}={e}^{-x}[(5Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x-(5P+Q)\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x]+x+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(0)=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n5Q-P+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo we have $P=3$ and $Q=1.$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡5x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡5x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{x}{2}-1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aded43d2-23a2-5e19-9ca1-e180c23cb2bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Be^{3x} - 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087354} {:answer/id #uuid "b41b0a33-65b5-5d1e-aa0d-20825fe8bf3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Bxe^{-x} - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087355} {:answer/id #uuid "cacd7224-dd35-5b33-bd7c-6ae3b9db409b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Be^{3x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087356} {:answer/id #uuid "426bf0dc-2860-5082-8e50-beb9266788ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-4x} + Be^{3x} + x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087357} {:answer/id #uuid "61f37d4e-3e02-5eb7-ae90-c34e8bb9eecc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = Ae^{-x} + Bxe^{-x} + 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087358}] :answer-field/id #uuid "14054356-ff2f-545f-8033-5c1431d3a511" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087353}] :question/id #uuid "62a4e5ba-fa3d-5d0d-9956-72e1733faeec" :question/math-academy-id "q-93671" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} + \\frac{dy}{dx} - 12y =-12$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087873 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d3438d77-c314-5b0a-b1f1-9ae4a3f970b6" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Constant Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "335feeb7-1626-5acb-a58c-0358339a7b88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355116} {:answer/id #uuid "6b592210-c753-5ff9-9c34-8ef1af145014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355614} {:answer/id #uuid "79848504-e602-53d9-9a9d-226978d6bc9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+B{e}^{3x}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355727} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbc759f-b136-5dd3-b320-f9fe2b45cba8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355933} {:answer/id #uuid "ef35e9f6-8ca2-5414-8174-5a35e0078136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef35e9f6-8ca2-5414-8174-5a35e0078136" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356750} :answer-field/id #uuid "0603be84-0f83-59e1-81b0-550df6722dca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d86c7083-2294-58a9-8fb2-8327c435d348" :question/math-academy-id "q-93672" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+9y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3$ is a repeated root, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $0.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $0,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0+0+9\\alpha =3\\,⟹\\,\\alpha =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}+Bx{e}^{-3x}+\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "934ee56b-b088-5533-ad4c-e3032ce0c524" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404581} {:answer/id #uuid "058adaab-a69e-5815-b046-5007fd02b2b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404582} {:answer/id #uuid "a209fd47-aee2-5925-bf14-e92cdd3c3c4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404583} {:answer/id #uuid "36905ca1-84ae-504d-9cfb-76b204089ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=-2x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404584} {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d2cc0e9-db58-596d-8e97-547728c8fbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}_{p}=3x-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404585} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3a0d7ea-2403-5870-baf3-f1e3941ec6cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186404580}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "311b05cd-8042-5d05-b094-868c3c494826" :question/math-academy-id "q-93785" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x+4\n$$\n\nhas the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x}+Bx{e}^{-x}$, find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." :question/worked-solution "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$  and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+2\\alpha +(\\alpha x+\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives:\n\n$$\n\\alpha x+(2\\alpha +\\beta )=3x+4\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =3\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =3,\\,\\beta =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=3x-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357568 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087874 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d86b46c0-e30d-525b-b84e-f8296c78aa8b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Affine Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a4ed55d-fca3-5a1a-85aa-44e65f648b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355327} {:answer/id #uuid "55ec1844-c660-5eaf-8c7f-4f07bd6f97d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355431} {:answer/id #uuid "637aa6b7-1ffb-5de6-890e-e7d29913306b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355549} {:answer/id #uuid "846c5112-b915-5771-a0fe-0d1e16e6a962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-x+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355831} {:answer/id #uuid "e20a27bd-dac9-557e-90a5-5c14dcaa5eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e20a27bd-dac9-557e-90a5-5c14dcaa5eb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356640} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ed96197-af5b-5b12-bb2c-598105826db5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "431e20fe-7075-5147-8313-e3b2fa443237" :question/math-academy-id "q-93811" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=18x+3.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-6y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-2$ or $\\lambda =3,$ and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=0\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0-\\alpha -6(\\alpha x+\\beta )=18x+3\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n-6\\alpha x+(-\\alpha -6\\beta )=18x+3.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-6\\alpha =18\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ -\\alpha -6\\beta =3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-3,\\beta =0.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-3x.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+B{e}^{3x}-3x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371250 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087875 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "41bccbd5-3d0c-5f00-b506-97b8f83c5472" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE with Quadratic Forcing"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98457a6f-2eb2-5f65-b07b-9d0471a1ae7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371252} {:answer/id #uuid "22f7247c-bbec-57c3-a6cf-ab9990130c34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+2{x}^{2}-2x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371253} {:answer/id #uuid "b929420e-728d-5b68-af34-594441565487" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}-2{x}^{2}-x+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371254} {:answer/id #uuid "4b596bb7-1a0f-570c-9084-cef33b2445cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371255} {:answer/id #uuid "f8b0ac43-f548-5ff0-87db-415578f14416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=A{e}^{-x}+B{e}^{3x}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b596bb7-1a0f-570c-9084-cef33b2445cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371255} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7d23e8b-11b4-5ec1-a02e-b04b713d3e20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3d5d66cf-660f-56ca-bb31-77faaca8db97" :question/math-academy-id "q-93831" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=5{x}^{2}-4x+5.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x),$ which is the solution to the homogeneous equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+10y=0.\n$$\n\nWriting down and solving the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +10 & =0 \\\\ ({\\lambda }^{2}+6\\lambda +9)-9+10 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +3)^{2} & =-1 \\\\ \\lambda +3 & =\\pm \\text{i} \\\\ \\lambda  & =-3\\pm \\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo $\\lambda =-3\\pm \\text{i}$, and therefore the complementary function ${y}_{c}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{p}$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta ,\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=2\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute the derivatives into the differential equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n2\\alpha +6(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+10(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n10\\alpha {x}^{2}+(12\\alpha +10\\beta )x+(2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma )=5{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}10\\alpha =5\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 12\\alpha +10\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\alpha +6\\beta +10\\gamma =5\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =-1,\\gamma =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by \n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-3x}(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+B\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-x+1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186357730 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087876 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "13078271-6d31-5476-8a81-d4fd714a2644" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Initial Value Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49ace747-f647-5e16-8d49-73c1ef8270dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355322} {:answer/id #uuid "4eda03f4-ad2b-5e8a-8eeb-1bb716e473cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355368} {:answer/id #uuid "d055c881-3067-5a66-b865-90f679d234fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356486} {:answer/id #uuid "dfab3e2c-f0d8-5382-89ec-ba3b9230f2c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356621} {:answer/id #uuid "fd0d5f81-1162-5159-88c0-7944f7d48a70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4{e}^{-x}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356883}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d055c881-3067-5a66-b865-90f679d234fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186356486} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddaa2fc5-3a2c-5dc7-873c-f7e09062158e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357316}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cc6aef37-e2a2-539b-a568-364586603b78" :question/math-academy-id "q-94329" :question/problem "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(P\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+Q\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}+5y=-5{x}^{2}+x-5,\\,y(0)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(0)=4.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We find the constants $P$ and $Q$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- Substituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P-1 & =0 \\\\ P & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- To apply the condition ${y}^{′}(0)=4,$ we first differentiate $y$ to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & ={e}^{-x}[(2Q-P)\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+(-Q-2P)\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]-2x+1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand then we substitute ${y}^{′}(0)=4$ into the above to get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2Q-P+1 & =4 \\\\ 2Q-1+1 & =4 \\\\ Q & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny={e}^{-x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x)-{x}^{2}+x-1.\n$$"}]}
